<header><p class="st-kicker">other · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Pashalik of Timbuktu</h1><p class="st-meta">1591 CE – 1833 CE (approx.) · capital Timbuktu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">State created by the Moroccan conquest of Songhai in 1591, ruled from Timbuktu by pashas of the Arma, descendants of Moroccan soldiers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1591 an army sent by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judar-pasha">Judar Pasha</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tondibi">Tondibi</a>. The Moroccans occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and Djenne and set up a government under a pasha, intended to send gold and slaves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Moroccan soldiers married local women, and their descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Arma</a>, became a ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> who elected the pashas from their own ranks after about 1612. Allegiance to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a> faded after the mid-17th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> remained a centre of scholarship, and local historians wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a> and other chronicles in this period.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The pashalik weakened in the 18th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> raids and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bambara</a> expansion, and the pashas became little more than town governors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massina-empire">Massina Empire</a> in the 1820s and 1830s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Arma</a> families remain a distinct group in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-bend">Niger bend</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pashalik-of-timbuktu.jpg" alt="Pashalik of Timbuktu" width="320" height="317"><figcaption>Image: Omar-Toons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TombouctouPachalik_4.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1591 CE – 1833 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Timbuktu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Judar Pasha, Mahmud ibn Zarqun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Songhay and Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arma Pashalik, Pashalik of the Sudan, Arma state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1712&amp;focus=pashalik-of-timbuktu" aria-label="Pashalik of Timbuktu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4598 1555 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pashalik of Timbuktu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4918 1811L4999 1829M4918 1811L4873 1200M4918 1811L4888 1900"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4999 1829h0M4873 1200h0M4888 1900h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4918 1811h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4918 1811h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Timbuktu. <a href="/atlas?year=1712&amp;focus=pashalik-of-timbuktu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
